"""Bring build_app.py up to the current app.

build_app.py ships as checksummed parts, and re-splitting the whole 100 KB file
through the file API for every change is both slow and the exact operation that
truncated a push once already. So the parts stay at a known baseline and the
difference is carried here as line-range replacements, generated mechanically
rather than typed.

It is deliberately paranoid: it refuses to run unless the file it is handed is
the baseline it was generated against, and it refuses to finish unless the
result hashes to the app that was actually tested. A short read at either end
fails the build instead of quietly shipping a half-written site.

Regenerate this whole file (not another one after it) for the next change, so
there is only ever one hop from the parts to the app.
"""
import hashlib, os

BASE = '1f36455fa8e4f3a847289e9cf537ea11ab7ef58134813bff0c6bc398fb4813bd'
WANT = 'e31b6470b8a23dbb1f729dff7518cbd50e0771c2c330db0cd60e2ce11f729da7'
path = os.path.join(os.environ.get('UBROOT', '.'), 'build_app.py')
src = open(path, encoding='utf-8').read()
got = hashlib.sha256(src.encode()).hexdigest()
assert got == BASE, 'build_app.py is not the baseline this was built from: ' + got

# (first line, last line + 1, replacement) against the baseline's own line
# numbering, applied back to front so earlier ranges stay valid.
